---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methodology — AI Governance Toolkit"
  description="How the AI Governance Toolkit was built, its standards alignment, and the principles behind it."
>
  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 lg:px-8 py-12">
    <p class="text-sm font-medium text-accent-700">Methodology</p>
    <h1 class="font-display font-semibold text-3xl md:text-4xl tracking-tight text-ink-900 mt-2">How the toolkit is built</h1>

    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-prose-wide mt-8">
      <h2>Standards alignment</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>ISO/IEC 42001:2023 — AI Management Systems</li>
        <li>ISO/IEC 23894:2023 — AI Risk Management</li>
        <li>ISO/IEC 42005 — AI System Impact Assessment</li>
        <li>NIST AI Risk Management Framework 1.0</li>
        <li>EU AI Act (Regulation (EU) 2024/1689)</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Author</h2>
      <p>Authored by a Candidate Expert on Canada's mirror committee for ISO/IEC JTC 1/SC 42. Mappings reflect direct reading of the published standards.</p>
      <h2>Design principles</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Privacy by design.</strong> All data stays in your browser. No backend.</li>
        <li><strong>Audit-defensible.</strong> Outputs are structured to feed the AI Controls Catalog's Test of Operating Effectiveness procedures.</li>
        <li><strong>Practical, not academic.</strong> Calibrated for a Friday afternoon, not a year-long consulting engagement.</li>
        <li><strong>Open and free.</strong> CC-BY 4.0 content, MIT code.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Limitations</h2>
      <p>This toolkit is a reference, not legal or regulatory advice. Specific engagements should validate framework references against current published versions and confirm with appropriate counsel.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
